<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    The console's Notification Service Extension code-signing entitlements — the console
    analog of the wallet's Entitlements.NSE.plist, whose header carries the full story of
    how this file reaches a shipped build (Xcode-driven signing via the template's
    CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS; the release lane's `_reexport-embedded-extensions` +
    `_verify-signed-ipa` pair in just/ios.just).

    ONE entitlement: the console's stable keychain group, where the app writes the
    notification private key and the NSE pin mirror (`AccessibleAfterFirstUnlock`).
    Without it the extension's unqualified Keychain reads search only its own implicit
    group, find nothing, and every push renders as the unverified notice.

    Unlike the wallet's NSE this lists NO legacy group: the console's notification items
    are new with this extension and have only ever lived in the stable group, so there is
    nothing legacy to read. And no `aps-environment` (an app entitlement) or cloud
    container, as ever — least privilege.

    PORTAL WORK: the extension is a separate bundle
    (dev.malpercio.admincompanion.NotificationService), so it needs its own explicit App
    ID and distribution profile (the IOS_MOBILE_PROVISION_ADMIN_NSE secret) before the
    admin TestFlight lane can sign a build that embeds it. See docs/ios-cicd.md.
  -->
  <key>keychain-access-groups</key>
  <array>
    <string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)org.obsign.admin.shared</string>
  </array>
</dict>
</plist>
